I am working to create a recipe book application. We are doing some spitballing before we start anything and I'd like your input.

Some things we need to discuss:

1) What is a recipe book application?
2) What do we want the interface do be like?
3) What features do we want?
4) What differentiates it from other recipe book apps?
5) What is our monetization policy and goals?

------------------

For me a recipe book application is more than just something to write ingredients and a structure. We want to be able to explore the ingredients and techniques.

Which brings me to the interface. I want to look like a rolodex or an old recipe book tin with the papers in it. When the user uses the volume buttons or scrolls up or down the recipes scroll through. I want a hamburger menu button on the top left that slides open a drawer with your menu options on the left. 

For features, I want the user to be able to enter their ingredients and it becomes a clickable item. More on that in a bit. But the clickable item ingredients are at the top, and the instructions near the bottom.

I want to be able to scale recipes for the user, and use a database to help the user convert from volumetric to mass for their recipes. And back if they need to.

I want to have an option for the user to click the ingredient, and it will pull up a flavour profile. The aromas, the flavours (sweet, spicy, salty, bitter, umami). I want to create almost a neural network of flavours, aromas, and textures that we can cross reference.

I want the user to be able to use this for substitutions.

I want to profile the users recipes that they have, and be able to generate recipes they may like from ingredients they frequently use. Perhaps a tally to mark how many times they've used the recipe so popularity can be tracked.

The ability to import from other popular recipe apps is important too, but that can be backburner for a long while.

Techniques and cooking methods can be integrated and marked on the recipes. We can use tags so recipes could be marked for "Wok" or "Deep-fried" or "Fermented", as well as search by ingredients the user wants to use.

Finally, I want to have a free ad-supported tier, and paid tier--a one-time purchase of 5.99 to remove ads forever.

I would like your input on this and your thoughts and criticisms.
